  <!-- ── OVERVIEW + DEFINITION ── -->
  <section class="features-section">
    <div class="section-inner" style="max-width:760px;">
      <span class="section-label">Overview</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">A new category of software</h2>
      <p class="integrations-desc" style="max-width:none;">
        In modern software design, system engineering, and digital product strategy, the distinction
        between a static product, a background service, and an operational workflow is rapidly
        disappearing. Traditional development models treat database records as passive data and
        backend pipelines as separate execution scripts. Modern scalable architecture demands a
        unified paradigm: every product type is fundamentally an entity with a lifecycle.
      </p>
      <p class="integrations-desc" style="max-width:none;">
        Whether you're building an e-commerce order engine, a fintech onboarding pipeline, an IoT
        telemetry stream, or an AI-driven automation hub, your domain components don't exist in
        isolation. They are stateful, event-driven objects that live, react, mutate, and evolve
        through structured states over time. Managing them well deserves its own category of software.
      </p>
      <div class="feat-card" style="cursor:default; border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:4px;">
        <div class="feat-desc" style="font-size:.95rem; color:var(--text-2);">
          <strong style="color:var(--text);">Entity Lifecycle Management (ELM)</strong> is software
          that stores data together with the flow that governs it. It manages any business entity
          from creation to completion — its attributes, its current state, the rules that move it
          forward, and the full history of how it got there.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

