  <!-- ── PART 1: FOUNDATIONS ── -->
  <section class="features-section">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <span class="section-label">Part 1 — Conceptual Foundations of ELM</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">The two pillars of ELM</h2>
      <p class="integrations-desc" style="max-width:none;">
        To design scalable platforms, software architects must bridge the gap between static
        domain models and asynchronous business logic. ELM unifies these through two pillars:
      </p>
      <div class="feat-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);">
        <div class="feat-card">
          <span class="feat-icon"><i class="bi bi-box-seam"></i></span>
          <div class="feat-title">Entities — the contextual anchor</div>
          <div class="feat-desc">Domain objects — user accounts, physical assets, transactional records, subscription profiles — that hold state, attributes, configuration parameters, and history.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <span class="feat-icon"><i class="bi bi-diagram-3"></i></span>
          <div class="feat-title">Flows — the engine of state execution</div>
          <div class="feat-desc">The deterministic, event-driven logic, transition rules, and integration loops that govern how an entity moves from one state to the next in response to internal or external triggers.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="integrations-desc" style="max-width:none; margin-top:2rem;">
        When entities and flows are coupled into a single, standardized abstraction, systems
        become inherently predictable, audit-ready, and modular. Developers no longer write
        fragmented glue code across multiple services to track an item's status — lifecycle
        management is built directly into the object's definition.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

